class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" color="#0000FF" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 124.33 211.95 L 109.4 193.04"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.61964 -0.78488 0.78488 -0.61964 112.29 196.69)"><path d="M 12.22 0 L 1.79 3.89 L 4.93 0 L 1.79 -3.89 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" color="#FF0000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 99.21 170.5 L 108.63 66.85"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.09055 -0.9959 0.9959 0.09055 108.21 71.49)"><path d="M 12.22 0 L 1.79 3.89 L 4.93 0 L 1.79 -3.89 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" color="#008000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 105.13 173.64 L 183.15 109.66"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.77327 -0.63408 0.63408 0.77327 179.55 112.61)"><path d="M 12.22 0 L 1.79 3.89 L 4.93 0 L 1.79 -3.89 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" color="#0000FF" stroke-width="0.8pt"><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 102.23 52.55 L 55.72 71.93"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.92308 0.38461 -0.38461 -0.92308 60.02 70.14)"><path d="M 12.22 0 L 1.79 3.89 L 4.93 0 L 1.79 -3.89 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" color="#0000FF" stroke-width="0.8pt"><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 192.97 106.18 L 165.4 161.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.44722 0.89442 -0.89442 -0.44722 167.48 157.16)"><path d="M 12.22 0 L 1.79 3.89 L 4.93 0 L 1.79 -3.89 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of actions which push the state around known operation regions of the <span id="S4.F4.sf2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">grl</span> agent, in the <math id="S4.F4.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\eta_{1}}-\overline{\eta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\eta_{1}}-\overline{\eta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> plane. An initial blue action causes a transition to \raisebox{-0.9pt}{1}⃝. From here a red action moves the state to the blue (known) operating region, a green action has the same effect – see \raisebox{-0.9pt}{2}⃝. The blue agent can then react which results in a state transition, again, to known operation regions, guaranteeing a valid defensive action.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrations of state (graph embedding) transitions in the latent space resulting from agent actions. <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> illustrates a scenario wherein following an action by a blue agent, subsequent actions by the red or green agents can transition the state to positions outside the observed data distribution. <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> depicts actions that keep the network state within known operating regions, illustrating how the blue agent’s defensive actions can ensure valid state transitions within the latent space.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key challenge with our approach lies in ensuring that the learned projections of network states align with the distribution of the observed data, as discrepancies (i.e. discontinuities) could prevent effective decision-making. Consider the scenario illustrated in <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> where, from an initial state, the blue agent takes an action that causes the state of the network to transition to a new position in the latent space – see \raisebox{-0.9pt}{1}⃝ in <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>. From here, in the next round, assume that either a red or green action, \raisebox{-0.9pt}{2}⃝ in <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>, results in a transition to the state of the network such that the new state is outside the observed network topology data, \raisebox{-0.9pt}{3}⃝. In such a scenario, a graph representation learning agent (or <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">blue</em> agent) cannot provide guarantees for the defense of a network as the new observation embedding lies in a region of space that it has not been trained to defend. Instead, any further action by the blue agent can result in further deviations of the state of the topology away from observed regions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Objective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To deal with the challenges discussed in the previous section, we construct objectives that rely on the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">mdh</span> being true (see the start of <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). In encoder-decoder-based <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gnn</span>s, the generator’s primary objective is to approximate the true data distribution, ensuring that its generated samples are located in the lower-dimensional data manifold. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}\subset\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}\subset\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be distributed as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the true data distribution, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all undirected graphs. The encoder <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to encode the data manifold from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to the latent space <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decoder <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> samples from the latent distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and maps these back to the data manifold <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="114.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 313.01 114.21" width="313.01"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,114.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(104.93,0) translate(0,98.64)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -100.32 -3.21)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="43.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 31.36 -3.21)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="40.43"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 33.95 -86.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.55em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="35.24"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 160.3 -3.21)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="43.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma_{\textsc{gt}},\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 157.71 -86.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="34.25"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -52.27 0 L 25.91 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 26.19 0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -40.11 -8.26)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 21.91)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="54.04"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Encoder </span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 19.22)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 20.1 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.03em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="14.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 76.67 0 L 154.86 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 155.14 0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 89.02 -5.8)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.88em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="53.66"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Decoder </span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.57)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 20.61 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.93em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="12.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 41.46 -12.06 C 26.84 -29.61 26.8 -53.14 41.31 -70.69"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.64551 -0.76375 0.76375 0.64551 41.49 -70.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.58 -46.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.72em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 61.47 -71.11 C 76.22 -53.67 76.42 -30.13 62.04 -12.49"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(-0.64008 0.76833 -0.76833 -0.64008 61.86 -12.27)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 77.57 -46.62)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.72em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 74.08 -82.92 L 152.27 -82.92"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 152.54 -82.92)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 92.6 -90.07)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 22.22)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.04em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="42.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Policy </span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 18.26)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.24 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="15.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.F5.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Commutative diagram showing the constituent objects and morphisms required to train a blue agent policy within the encoder-decoder-<span id="S4.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> framework. In each tuple the distribution is the first argument followed by the domain.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The distributions used in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, as well in the rest of this section, are explained below.</p>
<dl id="S4.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Underlying ground-truth distribution of data in the latent space <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> (theoretical and unknown)</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical distribution of latent codes <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by encoding the finite set of graphs (the actual discrete target for <span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdot</span>, defined overleaf)</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learned continuous latent distribution, constructed by pushing forward a simple noise distribution through the extended <span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdot</span> map</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uniform noise distribution <math id="S4.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> supported on <math id="S4.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\triangleq[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>≜</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\triangleq[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To get a handle on the meaning and purpose of these distributions, we have provided an additional analogy in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Understanding the distributions in the commutative diagram ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mode collapse and mixture</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The encoder-decoder approach described in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is well understood but suffers from <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mode collapse</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mode mixture</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagarajan &amp; Kolter, 2017</a>)</cite>. Mode collapse occurs when the generator fails to capture the diversity of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing limited or repetitive outputs by focusing only on a few high-density regions while ignoring others. This leads to a disconnected or sparse latent space (see e.g. <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> where the domain is not fully covered by the colored sub-spaces), where large portions of the input map to the same or very few outputs, creating discontinuities that result in abrupt jumps between generated samples rather than smooth transitions. In contrast, mode mixture leads to unrealistic interpolations between different data modes, causing blurry boundaries in the latent space. Instead of forming well-separated clusters, the latent representations overlap, blending multiple modes and failing to preserve distinct categories. Essentially, generative models struggle with mode collapse and mixture because they try to use neural networks to estimate mappings that are fundamentally discontinuous.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Optimal transport to the rescue</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimal transport (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Solomon, 2018</a>)</cite> has been suggested as a way of dealing with mode collapse and mode mixture. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2019)</a></cite> introduce the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ae</span>-<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> model (top part of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to deal with the above issues. They use an autoencoder (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ae</span>) to embed images (we embed graphs) in a low-dimensional manifold, while preserving important features upon which they employ optimal transport to map from a random noise distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, to the latent manifold <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, in a way that matches the distribution of the embedded real images.
We use similar ideas to embed attributed graphs <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and treat the resulting latent manifold as the state-space <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in a <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">pomdp</span> (see policy morphism in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As seen in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we employ two transport maps <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> to both map into the noise distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> as well as out of it. This allows us to use the samples in the noise distribution as a representation for our observations by mapping an observed sample from latent space to the noise space with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. As this space is structured to accurately model the observed samples, we can ensure that the resultant embedding would be an experience that the agent is trained to defend.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regular transport map</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within the latent manifold <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, latent codes <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=\{\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\mid i\in[T]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=\{\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\mid i\in[T]\}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, will typically cluster into different modes. Latent codes are mapped to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> using transport map</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}:\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}\xrightarrow{h_{\psi}}\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">ψ</mi></msub></mover><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}:\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}\xrightarrow{h_{\psi}}\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">shown on the left in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We let the transformation function <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> be a feedforward neural network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">McCulloch &amp; Pitts, 1943</a>)</cite> with parameters <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Semi-discrete optimal transport map</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2020)</a></cite>, we seek a continuous latent distribution <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> as seen in <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Discontinuous latent space ‣ 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> – i.e. a latent space without discontinuities. To be effective, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> must fulfill a set of desiderata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2019</a>)</cite>:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> has to properly generalize the true empirical latent distribution <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\nu}_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that all modes are covered by its support <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The support of <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> must have a topology similar to that of the ground truth <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that the map from <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> has to be efficient to sample from.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The semi-discrete optimal transport (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdot</span>) map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> fulfills all desiderata. Taking our cue from the original work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2019)</a></cite> we expose all steps that eventually lead to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We begin by making the observation that the target of the transportation is a discrete set of points <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding target measure, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, is represented as a Dirac measure for each discrete point: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\sum_{i=1}^{T}\nu_{i}\delta(\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{z}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\sum_{i=1}^{T}\nu_{i}\delta(\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{z}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the mass or probability associated with each point <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, the <em id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total mass</em> of the source and target measures must be equal: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\Omega)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\Omega)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a semi-discrete transport map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:\Omega\to\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:\Omega\to\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied, it naturally induces a decomposition of the source domain <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> into distinct cells <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{1},W_{2},\dots,W_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{1},W_{2},\dots,W_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\bigcup_{i=1}^{T}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\bigcup_{i=1}^{T}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Within this setup, every point <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> located in a specific cell <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped directly to its corresponding target point <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means the map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> effectively assigns all points within <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to be considered measure-preserving (denoted <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\#}\mu=\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\#}\mu=\nu</annotation></semantics></math>), the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-volume (or probability mass) of each cell <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must be equal to the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>-measure (mass) of its image, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(W_{i})=\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(W_{i})=\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cost function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santambrogio, 2015</a>, §7.2.2)</cite> is given by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\Omega\times\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\Omega\times\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(x,\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(x,\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cost of transporting a unit mass from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The total cost of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\Omega}c(x,\tau(x))\textrm{d}\mu(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\int_{W_{i}}c(x,\mathbf{z}_{i})\textrm{d}\mu(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\Omega}c(x,\tau(x))\textrm{d}\mu(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\int_{W_{i}}c(x,\mathbf{z}_{i})\textrm{d}\mu(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A common choice for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean distance, but our setting demands that we use a distance more suited to measuring the distance between two graphs <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ the Fused Gromov-Wasserstein (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</span>) distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vayer et al., 2018</a>)</cite> which measures similarities between two attributed graphs (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Fused Gromov-Wasserstein distance ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for more details). The <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdot</span> is the measure-preserving map that minimizes the total cost:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau^{*}=\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\tau_{\#\mu}=\nu}\int_{\Omega}c(x,\tau(x))\textrm{d}\mu(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}=\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\tau_{\#\mu}=\nu}\int_{\Omega}c(x,\tau(x))\textrm{d}\mu(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">piece-wise linearly extended to a global continuous map</em> <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. The details can be found in the original paper (incl. optimization details) but we will dwell on the key idea here owing to its importance to this work. While <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> maps an entire cell <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a single point <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the latent codes and the structure of the cells <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to create a continuous mapping – by filling in the ‘gaps’.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The gaps are filled by considering the latent codes, they are connected to form a mesh or simplicial complex<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              A simplicial complex is the result of tiling or approximating a space using triangles and tetrahedrons (in two and three dimensions respectively), in a way that preserves topological properties like holes, connectivity and boundaries.</span></span></span>. As the simplicial space preserves topological properties, the singularity set in the source domain <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> can be located and avoided when generating new graphs. Consequently, random noise from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> can be used in conjunction with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>, to generate new and valid graphs through <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}\circ\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}\circ\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Simpler still: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> finds the optimal way to distribute continuous noise to the specific discrete latent modes. It tells us which regions of the noise map to which latent mode. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> builds on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>; it takes the optimal cell decomposition and the latent codes and extends these into a continuous piecewise linear map. This allows for generating a continuum of diverse samples from the noise distribution, avoiding abrupt jumps at mode boundaries and provides a direct map from any random noise input to a meaningful latent code for generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Synthesizing a non-stationary environment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G_{t}\}_{t\in[T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G_{t}\}_{t\in[T]}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the graph topologies of a non-stationary environment – <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may change as <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> evolves. A priori, we do not have direct access to these topologies. However, we assume knowledge of the magnitude <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> of the target infrastructure (i.e., the number of nodes), which allows us to naively generate a large number of undirected graphs<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            There are a total of <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\frac{N(N-1)}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\frac{N(N-1)}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> undirected graphs in <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</span></span></span> using e.g. the Erdős–Rényi model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erdos et al., 1960</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Often, we can leverage domain-specific knowledge to better understand how the topology may evolve based on its design and the functionality of the attached nodes. This knowledge allows us to synthesize more accurate undirected graphs that are generated according to environmental rules. To achieve this, we introduce a procedural scenario generator (<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">psg</span>), described in <a href="#alg1" title="In Appendix C Procedural scenario generator algorithm ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the supplement, which generates scenarios resembling those in the <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cc2</span> environment illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Examples of generated networks are shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.1 Embedding observations ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. For further details, refer to <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Procedural scenario generator algorithm ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> of the supplement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Training the blue agent</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted at the start of this section, we present three model variations, indexed by the terms in the loss function.</p>
<dl id="S4.I3" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S4.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I3.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: <math id="S4.I3.ix1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">mse</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A modified <span id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">mpnn</span> implementation that enforces <span id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> on the latent space. The encoding model is trained end-to-end with the <span id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, with both the reward and transformation errors arising from <math id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> incorporated into the training loss.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I3.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: <math id="S4.I3.ix2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">mse</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implements a Graphormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ying et al., 2021</a>)</cite> or graph transformer model (<span id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gtm</span>) architecture to improve the expressiveness of the agent, augmenting it with the <span id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> mapping. In this case, the <span id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gtm</span> model is trained end-to-end with the <span id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> agent, with both the reward and transformation errors from <math id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> incorporated into the training loss.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I3.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: <math id="S4.I3.ix3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ae}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}};\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ae</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">mse</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ae}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}+\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}};\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uses the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gtm</span> model in an auto-encoding setup which is first pre-trained using the sum of the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> cost associated with the transformation <math id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">mse</span> loss associated with the transformation <math id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the reconstruction loss <math id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ae}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ae</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ae}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the graph samples, as formulated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kipf &amp; Welling (2016, Equation 3)</a></cite>. Once trained, the encoding layers of the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gtm</span> are used with the action network to construct the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gacd</span> agent. In this case, the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gtm</span> encoder’s weights are frozen, with the <span id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> agent being trained using the clipped loss formulation of <math id="S4.I3.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{ppo}}</annotation></semantics></math> as formulated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al. (2017, Equation 7)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With that we can write down losses associated with the <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span> segment of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.2 Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">mse</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{mse}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}=\|h_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-x_{i}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}=\|h_{\psi}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-x_{i}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\textsc{fgw}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c(x_{i},\tau(x_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c(x_{i},\tau(x_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.5 Training the blue agent ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> corresponds to the Monte-Carlo approximation of <a href="#S4.E3" title="In Semi-discrete optimal transport map ‣ 4.3 Optimal transport to the rescue ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The blue agent is trained as shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In Reward normalization procedure ‣ 4.5 Training the blue agent ‣ 4 Method ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, in a multi-task setting with each sampled environment representing a new task for the agent to learn. These environments are generated by the <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">psg</span> with a random red agent, two types of red agents are available in the <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cc2</span> scenario, assigned to each task. The blue agent operates on a batch of observations represented as a batched graph, each of which is projected into the random noise space <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, with the transformation map <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The embeddings in this uniform noise space represent the observations passed to the <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> algorithm which is comprised of two action networks, one identifying the host node which is acted upon by the blue agent and the second which identifies the action to be performed by the blue agent on the selected host node. The action that can be performed by the agent is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim P[h_{1}(x_{i}\mid\alpha)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim P[h_{1}(x_{i}\mid\alpha)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim P[h_{2}(x_{i}\mid\beta,n_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim P[h_{2}(x_{i}\mid\beta,n_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{1}(\cdot\mid\alpha):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}(\cdot\mid\alpha):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> represents an action network predicting energies <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over the nodes of the network; <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{2}(\cdot\mid\beta,n_{i}):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}(\cdot\mid\beta,n_{i}):\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a another action network that learns a distribution over the actions of the selected host. With the estimated energies, the probability of selecting a node that is acted upon is computed using a masked softmax operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The training is carried out for <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> time-steps, across ca. <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20,000</annotation></semantics></math> episodes. We train the agent on episodes having a maximum of <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>0 step length. To accommodate errors in the agent’s actions that result in failures to the simulation, we penalize such actions with a large negative reward <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1500)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1500)</annotation></semantics></math> truncating the episode when such failure is encountered. While training time is higher than the baseline Cardiff <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vyas et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, the training time per epoch is inline with other <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gnn</span> models. Note, in contrast to existing methods, our agent only needs to be trained <em id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">once</em> as it learns to generalize across network topologies. The inference time of all our agents are in the same order of magnitude as other <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> do not interact with each other and are trained independently, with a fixed set of topologies. <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same training procedure, while <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same parameter count. As the models do not interact with each other, there are no confounding factors across them.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward normalization procedure</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reward for the different environments are normalized to the baseline <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cc2</span> range, prior to being fed to the blue agent. We explored different normalization schemes but having analyzed the results, we concluded that it is not possible to compare the two settings. For example, a topology that has three operational servers can have three times worse reward than an topology with a single operational server, as each impact on the operational server can lead to a high penalty. Hence, there is a need to normalize the rewards to the same scale – chosen as described to the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cc2</span> range.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To train the agents, we combine the representation learning step, with that of training the agent in a <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ppo</span> setting, optimizing for the losses as described in previous section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
