<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Fused Gromov-Wasserstein distance</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vayer et al. (2018)</a></cite> suggest a paradigm for viewing graphs as probability distributions, embedded in a specific metric space. Notably, in their work they operate on attributed graphs which is to say structured data with both feature information as well as structure information. Optimal transport (<span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ot</span>) provides a principled framework for comparing probability measures <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> by determining the most efficient way to transform one into the other <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thorpe, 2018</a>)</cite>. Specifically, it seeks an optimal coupling –also known as a transport plan– that minimizes the total transport cost required to move mass from <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting minimal cost defines the optimal transport distance, which serves as a meaningful metric for comparing distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are interested in the distance between two graphs <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with associated feature sets <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, each described by their probability measure, as noted above:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu=\sum_{i=1}^{n}h_{i}\delta_{(x_{i},a_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu=\sum_{i=1}^{n}h_{i}\delta_{(x_{i},a_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu=\sum_{i=1}^{n}g_{j}\delta_{(y_{j},a_{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu=\sum_{i=1}^{n}g_{j}\delta_{(y_{j},a_{j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{i}\in\sum_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\sum_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{j}\in\sum_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}\in\sum_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are histograms, <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Dirac delta centered on <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi(h,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(h,g)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all admissible couplings (a way to describe a joint probability distribution over two spaces that respects given marginal distributions) between <math id="A2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, in other words the set:</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi(h,g)=\{\pi\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n\times m}s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{n}=\pi_{i,j}=h_{j};\sum_{j=1}^{m}=\pi_{i,j}=g_{i}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">.</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo lspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mo lspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(h,g)=\{\pi\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n\times m}s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{n}=\pi_{i,j}=h_{j};\sum_{j=1}^{m}=\pi_{i,j}=g_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the amount of mass shifted from bin <math id="A2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for coupling<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Intuition: given <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, a coupling tells you how to pair samples from <math id="footnote4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with samples from <math id="footnote4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <math id="A2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vayer et al., 2018</a>, §3)</cite>. Where we can construe <math id="A2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> as a matrix which describes the probabilistic matching of the nodes of <math id="A2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, let <math id="A2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{AB}=(d(a_{i},b_{j}))_{i.j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{AB}=(d(a_{i},b_{j}))_{i.j}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="A2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix which measures the distance between the features according to some distance <math id="A2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the structure matrices<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vayer et al. (2018)</a></cite> abuses notation somewhat, <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a function that measures the similarity of node attributes, <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is also the structure matrix which stores these similarities.</span></span></span> are denoted <math id="A2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=C(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\forall\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\in\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=C(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\forall\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\in\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=C(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\forall\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\in\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=C(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\forall\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\in\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Where <math id="A2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as we <math id="A2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the marginals of <math id="A2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Finally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vayer et al. (2018)</a></cite> define a 4-dimensional tensor which measures the similarity between two structure matrices i.e.</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i,j,k,l}=\|C_{1}(i,k)-C_{2}(j,l)\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i,j,k,l}=\|C_{1}(i,k)-C_{2}(j,l)\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We now have all the ingredients we need to define the Fused Gromov-Wasserstein distance which is defined given a trade-off parameter <math id="A2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and where the metric is defined for <math id="A2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math> (but is defined also for larger values but that discussion is outside the scope of this review):</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\textsc{fgw}}^{\alpha,q}=\min_{\pi\in\Pi(h,g)}E_{q}(M_{AB},C_{1},C_{2},\pi)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</mtext><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textsc{fgw}}^{\alpha,q}=\min_{\pi\in\Pi(h,g)}E_{q}(M_{AB},C_{1},C_{2},\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{q}(M_{AB},C_{1},C_{2},\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{q}(M_{AB},C_{1},C_{2},\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\langle(1-\alpha)M^{q}_{AB}+\alpha L(C_{1},C_{2})^{q}\otimes\pi,\pi\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\langle(1-\alpha)M^{q}_{AB}+\alpha L(C_{1},C_{2})^{q}\otimes\pi,\pi\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i,j,k,l}(1-\alpha)d(a_{i},b_{j})^{q}+\alpha\|C_{1}(i,k)-C_{2}(j,l)\|^{q}\pi_{i,j}\pi_{k,l}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i,j,k,l}(1-\alpha)d(a_{i},b_{j})^{q}+\alpha\|C_{1}(i,k)-C_{2}(j,l)\|^{q}\pi_{i,j}\pi_{k,l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A2.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">fgw</span> distance jointly exploits both features and structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Procedural scenario generator algorithm</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To create structural variations in the <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cc2</span> environment network topology, we employ a procedural scenario generator (<span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">psg</span>) that randomly synthesized new environments following a set of rules with which to guide the environment dynamics. <a href="#alg1" title="In Appendix C Procedural scenario generator algorithm ‣ General Autonomous Cybersecurity Defense:Learning Robust Policies for Dynamic Topologies and Diverse Attackers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the steps involved in the <span id="A3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">psg</span> process, where we randomly sample the number of subnets, the number of hosts on each subnet and the assignment of the agents to subnets hosts based on an input of the upper and lower bounds for these. The generator creates and returns the graph representation of the network based on the configurations provided.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Procedural scenario generator</figcaption>
