<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Understanding the distributions in the commutative diagram</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imagine you want to draw a map of all the cities in the USA.</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A4.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="A4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This would be the true, ideal probability distribution of <span id="A4.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all actual human settlements</span> across the USA (cities, towns, villages, etc.), which is complex and includes lots of clusters and empty spaces. You can never perfectly know this.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A4.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{\textsc{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{\textsc{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="A4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the <span id="A4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actual data</span> – the specific locations of the 100 largest cities you have in your dataset. This is a finite and discrete list of points.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A4.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="A4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the learned “map” of settlement density. We take a simple grid (the initial noise <math id="A4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> for the <span id="A4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdot</span>) and warp it such that the density on the warped map corresponds to the locations of the 100 cities. This warped map <math id="A4.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> now has high-density areas where cities are and low-density areas in between, accurately reflecting the real distribution, even though it is still a continuous “map”. Now, if we give this smart, warped map to someone to ‘fill in the details’ (our neural-net model), they will do a much better job than if we just gave them a blank, uniform grid.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
